---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { taxYears } from "../data";

/** "gov.uk/government/publications/…" reads better than the full URL. */
const short = (url: string) => {
  const u = new URL(url);
  return `${u.hostname.replace(/^www\./, "")}${u.pathname.replace(/\/$/, "")}`;
};
---
<Base title="How it works" description="What the calculators assume, and where the numbers come from.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Assumptions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pay is assumed to be the same every pay period, so tax and National Insurance are worked out on the yearly total.</li>
      <li>National Insurance is Class 1, category A (most employees). Tick "Over State Pension age" for no employee National Insurance.</li>
      <li>Without a tax code, the standard Personal Allowance is used. It is reduced by £1 for every £2 of income over £100,000. If you enter a tax code, it replaces this.</li>
      <li>Undergraduate student loan plans share one repayment, worked out on the lowest threshold you hold. Postgraduate loans are repaid separately.</li>
      <li>Pensions: <strong>salary sacrifice</strong> reduces pay before tax, National Insurance and student loan. <strong>Net pay</strong> reduces pay before tax only. <strong>Relief at source</strong> is taken after tax: you pay 80% and your provider adds 20%. Higher-rate relief claimed through Self Assessment is not shown.</li>
      <li>Pay variants (beta) works out each deduction on both amounts as if every pay period were paid at that rate, like a Week 1/Month 1 tax code. On a cumulative tax code, a one-off correction can change tax by a different amount. Without a tax code, the allowance taper applies to the yearly figure, so above about £100,000 a year the tax adjustment can be higher than payroll would take.</li>
      <li>Results are estimates. Your payslip may differ slightly because of rounding and how your employer runs payroll.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    {[...taxYears].reverse().map((y) => (
      <>
        <h3>{y.label} tax year</h3>
        <ul>{y.sources.map((s) => <li><a href={s} rel="noopener">{short(s)}</a></li>)}</ul>
      </>
    ))}
  </article>
</Base>
